cat > logs/build/phaseB-review.md <<'EOF'
# Phase B review of Phase A

Reviewed SPEC.md, research/NOTES.md, Phase A at 58fd322, all eight skill entrypoints and helpers, and the available upstream snapshots. A delegated reviewer performed a read-only static pass; the main agent implemented fixes and ran all commands serially. No network, remote, Docker, or external project was used.

## Contracts and provenance

All eight original frontmatters already matched their directory names and had nonempty descriptions with Use and the slash invocation. Required body contracts and source IDs were present: verification interview/scaffold/live proof, maintenance outcomes and edit scope, correction pyramid and rule table, feedback provenance and historical replay, distinct architectural candidates, brief/outside check, formal able-to-fail and findings-stay-findings, curator cadence and acceptance bar. No wholesale prose rewrite was needed.

S20 remains explicitly secondary. S21 was incorrectly called secondary/fetched although only a search snippet had been seen; changed it to unverified. No skill drew claims from S21. Skills remain tied to the recorded S1..S23 evidence; the offline curator fixture makes no claim to current web research.

## Findings and fixes

1. **Ratchet reset loophole (high):** patlint omitted zero-count adopted rules. A later update could re-adopt new violations. Preserve empty rule entries. The example runner adopts a violation, rejects growth, lowers to zero, rejects reintroduction, and checks valid/expired exceptions with a fixed test date.
2. **False feedback proof (high):** a failed fixed-revision replay counted as zero, and any decrease was described as no hit on the fix. Require a successful bad replay with hits and a successful fixed replay with zero hits. A regression simulates a failed replay and asserts unproven.
3. **Overbroad generated lint and wrong owner (high):** diff-token voting selected innocent serialization and reads; Store.add inferred Store.py rather than todo/store.py. Resolve Python classes/functions to their actual tracked source. Replay each candidate before promotion and retain only patterns distinguishing the actual mistake from the fix. Existing HEAD violations remain available for ratcheting. Generated rules fail on the real past direct-write commit and pass HEAD; generated REFACTOR_PLAN.md retains the replay evidence.
4. **False clean drift (medium):** removing every HTTP route suppressed dead-route checks; deleting the whole Python CLI removed its detection vocabulary. Check mapped routes against even an empty source inventory and recover Python/node invocation forms from map recipes. Regressions cover deleted map routes, newly added app routes, removal of all HTTP routes, and removal of the CLI.
5. **Prose mistaken for enforcement (medium):** README prose containing the word tests counted as a check. Classify prose before free-text check keywords. The real example flags exactly one prose-only rule, and README-documents-the-tests is rejected.
6. **Bytecode invalidated a verification skill (medium):** cached .pyc files were treated as executable helper scripts. Ignore bytecode and __pycache__. Compile a generated Python helper and confirm the contract still passes; a damaged feature heading still fails.
7. **Cleanup scope and PID identity (high):** the generated harness signalled a process group and used second-resolution start timestamps. Signal only the recorded PID, require a nonempty matching Linux boot/start identity, and treat zombies as stopped. Update generated and skill prose accordingly. The live runner checks server shutdown and survival of an unrelated owned sentinel. Full launch/doctor/drive/evidence/cleanup execution remains unverified here because the sandbox rejects sockets before launch; no runtime success is claimed.
8. **Curator false success and offline network (high):** upstream failure became an empty report and then a clean proposal; --offline could still fetch. Offline now forbids fetches and uses a populated cache, and failed diffs terminate the run. Regression asserts no proposal appears after failure. A fake initialized upstream with no remotes exercises changed-file and first-run new-skill reports, proposal generation, validation, and logs.
9. **Curator source/commit gates (high):** HTTP 401/403/429 passed fetched-source validation, online apply could reuse proposals without refetching, and an environment variable could replace verification with an arbitrary command. Require successful fetched responses, refetch on online apply, and always run scripts/verify.sh --run-examples before committing. Malformed proposal text must not pass as an empty list. HTTP rejection regressions stub only transport to avoid network; real offline proposal/diff commands run normally. Online apply is not executed in this offline task.
10. **Snapshot provenance (medium):** offline apply changed snapshots without recording the new source head/version. Append exact files, source URLs, head and version to research/snapshot-syncs.json and link it from NOTES. Shell syntax and packaging checks cover this path; no apply branch was created during this task.
11. **Model checker false or unusable results (medium):** dataclasses with postponed annotations failed import, empty initial states passed, invalid bounds were accepted, and deadlocks counted as killing invariant mutants. Register imported models, validate initial states/bounds, and require an invariant violation to kill a mutant. The actual dataclass todo model exhausts its finite state space; unlocked writers, skipped done, and repeated done each produce counterexamples. Regressions reject empty models, negative depth and deadlock-only mutants. Missing TLA+ correctly reports exit 1 and the stdlib fallback; this is availability information, not a checker failure.

## Phase B result and remaining environment constraint

The deterministic builder reproduces the same nested HEAD in independent directories. Its ten commits span September–October 2026, preserve two direct JSON writes, a revert, two status bugs and their fixes, and retain review/chat evidence. Seven HEAD unit tests and the persistence boundary lint pass.

The example runner and collection verifier are implemented, with real expected-failure assertions, per-skill logs and committed artefacts. Six skills pass. create-verification-skill and maintain-verification-skill remain failed because the execution sandbox denies socket creation: PermissionError [Errno 1] Operation not permitted in socket.socket(), before any server can start. There is no available approval escalation. The assertions were not weakened, skipped or replaced with mocked HTTP. No live request/response evidence is fabricated; the generated skill and failure output are retained. The overall verifier exits nonzero on those failures.

Re-run scripts/run-examples.sh and scripts/verify.sh where localhost sockets are permitted to complete the live acceptance proof. This limitation prevents an honest all-green Phase B claim in the current environment.
